---
// @source S-061 (Birch-Murnaghan), S-062 (Murnaghan), S-068 (Levenberg-Marquardt), S-041 S-042 (E-V workflow)
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EosTool from '../../components/tools/eos/EosTool';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Equation of state fit"
  description="Fit energy-volume points to the Birch-Murnaghan or Murnaghan equation of state and read the equilibrium volume, bulk modulus and lattice constant. Runs in your browser."
  pageClass="tool"
>
  <div class="container">
    <EosTool client:load />
    <section class="card tool-background" aria-labelledby="eos-bg">
      <h2 id="eos-bg" class="card-h">
        Background
      </h2>
      <details>
        <summary>Why fit a curve instead of relaxing the cell?</summary>
        <p>
          A cell relaxation (ISIF = 3) follows the stress, and at a fixed plane-wave cutoff the stress carries
          an error called Pulay stress, so it needs about 30 % more cutoff to converge. Each point of an
          energy-volume curve is a run at one fixed volume, so the curve does not depend on the stress at all.
          Use the same ENCUT and k-mesh at every volume, and relax ions (ISIF = 2 for cubic cells) or ions and
          shape (ISIF = 4) at each one. The Input Builder's series step writes these folders for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take 7 to 11 volumes spread over 5 to 10 % on both sides of the minimum. Too few points or a narrow
          range make B0' unreliable; a minimum at the edge of the range means the scan has to be extended.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What the two forms are</summary>
        <p>
          Both forms describe E(V) with four numbers: the minimum energy E0, the equilibrium volume V0, the
          bulk modulus B0 and its pressure derivative B0'. Third-order Birch-Murnaghan expands the energy in a
          finite strain and is the usual default for solids. Murnaghan assumes B0' is constant with pressure;
          it works well close to V0 and less well far from it. Both forms share E0, V0, B0 and B0' at the
          minimum and differ only further away, so a large difference between the two fits means the scan
          reaches far from V0.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fit uses Levenberg-Marquardt least squares from a parabola start, the same recipe as pymatgen.
          The units are converted with 1 eV/Å³ = 160.21766 GPa.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .tool-background {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .tool-background details + details {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
